# Installing Node.js and npm

A **package manager** installs and manages the dependencies of your code: the libraries you did not write but your project needs. The one we will use is **npm**, and npm arrives with **Node.js**, so Node comes first.

## What Node.js Is

The Chrome browser is made of parts. **V8** is the JavaScript engine, the part that actually runs your JavaScript. **Blink** is the rendering engine that turns HTML into pixels. There is networking code, and a good deal more besides.

**Node.js** is what you get when you take V8 out of Chrome, add a set of libraries for things a browser deliberately will not do, such as reading files, and ship it as a plain desktop application.

The consequence is the important part: **JavaScript is no longer confined to a page.** The same language you have been writing runs at a command prompt, on a server, in a build tool. Every tool in the rest of this module is a JavaScript program run by Node.

## Installing It

If you are working in the Coursera environment, Node and npm are already installed and there is nothing to do.

To install it on your own machine, go to [nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org) and use **Get Node.js**. It gives you instructions for your platform, in the form of a few commands to paste into a console. Choose the **LTS** version, which stands for long term support: it is the one that will keep receiving fixes for a good while, and the one most tools expect.

Then check that it worked:

```
$ node --version
v22.19.0
```

Your number will differ, and that is fine. What matters is that you get a version rather than an error.

If you type just `node` with no arguments, you land somewhere unexpected:

```
$ node
Welcome to Node.js v22.19.0.
>
```

That `>` is an interactive prompt where you can type JavaScript a line at a time. It is useful for quick experiments, and it is not where you want to be when you meant to run a file. Get out with **Ctrl+D**, or **Ctrl+C twice**, or by typing `.exit`.

## Running a File

This is the part that changes how you work. Put some JavaScript in a file:

```javascript
// index.js
console.log('Hello Node');
```

Then, from the directory that file is in, run it:

```
$ pwd
/home/alice/code/hello
$ ls
index.js
$ node index.js
Hello Node
```

No HTML, no browser, no page. Note the `pwd` and `ls` first: `node index.js` looks for `index.js` in the working directory, so being in the wrong directory is the usual reason for a "cannot find module" complaint.

Cells marked `node` in these notebooks run with Node-style globals in place of browser ones, standing in for a real `node index.js`:

```{node autorun}
console.log('Hello Node');

console.log('this file is running in ' + process.cwd());
```

`process` is one of the globals Node provides and a browser does not. There is no `document` here and no page to touch, because there is no page.

## npm Comes With It

Installing Node also installs **npm**. Check it the same way:

```
$ npm --version
10.9.3
```

npm is a package manager: it installs libraries into your project, keeps track of which versions you are using, and lets you publish libraries of your own.

The name is a small joke. It originally stood for "node package manager", and officially it now stands for **"npm is not an acronym"**. The reason is probably that npm long ago stopped being only about Node: most of the browser libraries in this course, Lodash included, are distributed through npm too.

That is the setup done. The next readings are about using it.

## Exercises

1. Check that both tools are installed, then run a file with Node.

    ```{external}
    title: Node and npm setup checks
    interval: off
    - command: node --version | Print the Node.js version
    - command: npm --version | Print the npm version, which came bundled with Node
    - command: node index.js | Run a JavaScript file with Node
    ```
    ```+terminal
    welcome: Type each command and press Enter, then run the cell to check.
    prompt: $
    ```

2. Write the contents of an `index.js` that logs `Hello from Node` and then logs the working directory it was run from.

    ```{node}
    // index.js

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+solution
    // index.js

    console.log('Hello from Node');
    console.log(process.cwd());
    ```
    ```+test
    check.consoleLogged('Hello from Node').run('Log the exact text `Hello from Node`', 'Logged the greeting');
    check.consoleLogged('/workspace').run('Log the working directory as well, using `process.cwd()`', 'Logged the working directory');
    wrap(console.history().length).should('equal', 2).run('Two lines of output, the greeting and the directory', 'Logged both lines');
    ```

3. What is Node.js?

    ```{mcq}
    Which description fits Node.js?
    correct: Correct! It is Chrome's V8 engine, plus extra libraries, packaged so that JavaScript runs on your computer rather than only inside a page.
    wrong: Think about which part of the browser Node.js reuses, and where the code ends up running.
    [x] An environment that uses Chrome's V8 engine to run JavaScript on your computer
    [ ] A new programming language that replaces JavaScript
    [ ] A web browser built specifically for developers
    [ ] A tool for designing HTML and CSS layouts
    ```

4. You install Node.js and find you also have npm. What is npm for?

    ```{mcq}
    What job does npm do?
    correct: Correct! It is a package manager: it installs third-party libraries into your project, tracks their versions, and can publish your own.
    wrong: The clue is in the phrase "package manager". What is a package, and what would managing it involve?
    [x] Installing, sharing, and managing the third-party libraries your project depends on
    [ ] Compiling your JavaScript into another language so the computer can read it
    [ ] Providing a window where you drag and drop files
    [ ] Removing the need to use a console at all
    ```

